It means displaying different UI based on a condition.
Conditional rendering in React means rendering different UI elements based on a condition. 
React supports conditional rendering using JavaScript techniques such as if statements, logical AND operator, ternary operator, and early returns.

● if
Use if when have multiple conditions or more complex logic.
Use if when the condition decides which entire UI/component should be returned.

● AND Operator (&&)
&& is useful when want to show something only when a condition is true.
When to use && - Show this only if the condition is true.
Syntax :- {condition && <Component />}

If condition is TRUE → Show Component
If condition is FALSE → Show Nothing

true && JSX   → JSX will display
false && JSX  → JSX will NOT display

Examples :-
{isAdmin && <AdminPanel />}
{isLoggedIn && <LogoutButton />}
{hasNotifications && <NotificationIcon />}
{cartItems.length > 0 && <Checkout />}

● Ternary
It is useful when want to choose between two different UI elements.
Syntax :- condition ? True : False

condition
   ↓
 ┌───────┐
true    false
 ↓        ↓
 A        B

Examples :-
{isLoggedIn ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />}
{isLoading ? <Loading /> : <ProductList />}
{isDarkMode ? <DarkTheme /> : <LightTheme />}

● Early Return
It means checking a condition at the beginning of a component and returning immediately if that condition is met.
When a function or React component has multiple return statements, the first return whose condition becomes true will execute and immediately stop the function execution.
Even if multiple conditions are true, only the first matching return will execute because return stops the execution immediately there only.
Multiple returns → First matching return executes → Execution stops → Remaining returns are not executed.
It is especially useful for: Loading, Error, Authentication, Missing data, Permission checks.
